  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1212 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nPersistent northerly surface flow and an upper trof overhead will \nprovide near to slightly below normal temperatures and lower \nhumidity through Saturday. Winds will gradually shift back to \nthe south Saturday as a surface high over the Great Lakes region\nmoves eastward. Upper level ridging over the western half of \nthe U.S. will gradually shift eastward through the weekend into \nearly next week, moving overhead by mid week. This will keep the\nPAH forecast dry over the next week. By early next week, the \nmiddle Mississippi and lower Ohio valleys will be sandwiched \nbetween a surface high to our east and low pressure to our west.\nThis will result in increasing south to southwest flow across \nour region. A warming trend will begin Sunday and continue \nthrough next week, pushing high temperatures Monday through \nThursday into the middle to upper 90s, with some areas pushing \n100 degrees Wednesday and Thursday. Dew points will remain in \nthe upper 60s to around 70 degrees through the period, so heat \nindices will generally be 4 to 6 degrees above the actual \ntemperatures. The HeatRisk Map indicates our region in the \nmajor category by Tuesday and Wednesday, and major to extreme \nThursday. The duration of this heat wave may result in some heat\nheadlines next week.",
